{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001784","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates are direct matches for abdominal aortic aneurysm by trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported, so ranking hinges on PRS-comparable performance evidence and study/method signals.

PGS001784 is preferred because it reports an explicit PRS-comparable metric (Nagelkerke’s R² with covariates regressed out / PGS-only R² = 0.014661) in a European evaluation sample, which is the most reliable cross-model discriminator available in this set. Competing candidates either (a) lack PRS-only metrics entirely and report only full-model AUROC/C-index (PGS003429/PGS003972/PGS003973), which is not comparable for PRS ranking and can be covariate-driven, or (b) provide weaker/less standard PRS-comparable evidence (PGS002054 partial-r 0.0144) or only an effect size from a sparse 29-variant P+T score (PGS000753 OR 1.37 per SD, moderate).

Method/architecture also supports PGS001784: PRS-CS-auto with ~911k variants is a modern genome-wide approach and is structurally more likely to capture polygenic signal than the very small P+T score, while still providing a PRS-only metric unlike several newer disease-focused papers here that only expose full-model discrimination."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001260","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates map to the correct EFO (angina pectoris), but the most decision-relevant differentiator here is availability of PRS-comparable performance (PGS-only metrics) on a direct angina endpoint with basic covariates.

PGS001260 reports explicit PGS-only AUROC (0.58108) and PGS-only R² (0.01185) in European validation, which is the most comparable evidence for standalone PRS performance among the list. Competing UKB snpnet variants (PGS001261/PGS001262) have very similar PGS-only AUROC/R², but PGS001261 is time-to-event (endpoint packaging penalty) and PGS001262 uses a more questionnaire/doctor-diagnosed phrasing that can inflate full-model metrics; their small PGS-only differences are not meaningful.

Other candidates either lack PRS-only metrics entirely (PGS000703, PGS004457, PGS004527) or report only full-model C-index/OR without clear PRS-only discrimination (PGS005047–PGS005059), making them less directly comparable for selecting a single best PGS for generic angina pectoris risk."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002304","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"PGS002304 is the best direct match for the target trait (follicular lymphoma) because its trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both explicitly align to follicular lymphoma, whereas PGS000643/PGS000644 are labeled/phenotyped as “Nodular lymphoma” (despite mapping to follicular lymphoma in trait_efo), and PGS003455 has an internal phenotype inconsistency (phenotyping_reported = chronic lymphocytic leukemia) that undermines endpoint fidelity.

On the available performance evidence, PGS002304 also shows the strongest reported discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.61, European validation), compared with ~0.53–0.54 for PGS000643/PGS000644. Although these are full-model AUROCs (not PRS-only) and covariates are unknown for PGS002304 (so comparability is imperfect), the combination of best endpoint fidelity plus the highest reported AUROC makes it the most defensible choice in this fixed candidate set.

The recommendation is marked SUB_OPTIMAL because none of the candidates report clearly PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R², and PGS002304’s validation sample size is small (n=197), limiting confidence in generalizability."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002082","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Target trait is lupus erythematosus (umbrella). Candidates split between (a) systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) diagnosis and (b) restricted sub-phenotypes (early-onset SLE, renal disease, lupus nephritis class V, erythematous conditions) or portability-framework scores.

PGS002082 is selected because it best matches the umbrella target label (trait_efo = lupus erythematosus; phenotyping_reported = lupus localized and systemic) while also using a methodologically strong genome-wide approach (LDpred2) with a large variant set (361,553). Its reported metric is PRS-comparable (partial correlation) rather than a covariate-inflated full-model AUROC, and it slightly outperforms the other umbrella-lupus portability option (PGS001870) on the same endpoint and evaluation setting.

Why not the higher-AUROC SLE models: several SLE candidates report only full-model AUROC with unknown or clearly non-comparable covariates (e.g., PGS003960 includes a phenotype risk score covariate, making AUROC=0.89 not interpretable as PRS strength). Others have endpoint restrictions (early-onset only, renal disease onset, nephritis subtype) that are poorer matches to generic lupus erythematosus. Therefore, despite being from a portability framework paper (a mild negative), PGS002082 is the most endpoint-faithful and structurally strong choice available for the umbrella trait."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004606","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"PGS004606 is the strongest direct match for the target trait (macular degeneration) because it targets age-related macular degeneration with a direct disease endpoint (phenotyping_reported: Age-related macular degeneration) and comes from a recent disease-focused GWAS/meta-analysis context (Nat Genet 2024) using a modern genome-wide PRS method (PRS-CS) with a very large variant set (~1,000,946), which is a strong structural signal when PRS-only metrics are not broadly available across candidates.

Performance reporting is imperfect across the pool: only PGS001013 provides explicit PGS-only AUROC/R², but those are weak (PGS-only AUROC ~0.553; PGS-only R² ~0.006) and its phenotype is a UKB “Eye problems/disorders” label, suggesting a more generic/heterogeneous ascertainment. Several other candidates (PGS001834/PGS002041) report only partial correlation with covariates and no PRS-only discrimination metrics, making them hard to rank on performance.

Among the AMD-specific candidates, PGS004606 has cleaner endpoint alignment than PGS002269 (which is anchored to retinal layer thickness rather than AMD diagnosis) and stronger evidence than PGS004607 (trained on AFR GWAS but evaluated in EUR with lower reported full-model AUROC). PGS004952 reports an extremely high AUROC but only as a full-model metric in a very small validation sample (n=1,232) and with a likely reporting/scale inconsistency (AUROC shown as 84.2), so it is less reliable as the primary choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000136","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates except PGS002785 are direct matches on trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported (schizophrenia). PGS002785 is an endpoint mismatch (phenotyping_reported is cognitive function), so it is not suitable for schizophrenia risk.

Among the direct-match PRS-CS scores (PGS000133–PGS000136), none reports PRS-only AUROC/R²; only full-model AUROC and/or per-SD OR are available with similar basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, platform/batch). In this setting, effect size evidence is a useful differentiator. PGS000136 has a strong OR per SD (1.7) and a higher full-model AUROC (0.64) than the other large-validation options (0.60), while also having a reasonably large European validation sample (n=18,461). PGS000135’s higher AUROC (0.74) is based on a much smaller validation sample (n=9,569) and lacks effect-size support, making it less stable as the best choice.

Therefore, PGS000136 is the best available direct-match candidate, but the overall recommendation is sub-optimal because the pool lacks PRS-only performance metrics and training sample details."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000471","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all align to skin carcinoma in situ) and share the same publication context, EUR-only training/evaluation, identical covariate set (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), and the same reported validation size (n=5,500). None reports PRS-only AUC/R², so we must rely on the available full-model metrics and effect sizes within this otherwise tightly comparable cluster.

PGS000471 (lassosum, 7 variants) shows the strongest reported discrimination and PRS signal among the three: highest full-model AUROC (0.569) and Nagelkerke R² (0.0255), plus the largest per-SD effect size (OR 1.401, 95% CI 1.297–1.513) and strongest tail separation (OR top 1% vs rest 3.77). PGS000470 is intermediate (AUROC 0.557; OR per SD 1.308), and PGS000469 is weakest despite very large variant count (AUROC 0.524; OR per SD 1.09). Given identical endpoint and covariates, these differences are the best available basis for selection.

Outcome is marked sub-optimal because the catalog record lacks PRS-only performance metrics, so confidence in standalone PRS performance is limited even though the within-cluster comparison favors PGS000471."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000613","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait_efo urinary bladder carcinoma; phenotyping is bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / incident bladder cancer / urothelial carcinoma). Most records lack PRS-only metrics; many report only full-model AUROC, which is not reliably comparable across studies, especially when covariate sets differ.

Within the large coherent PRSweb cluster (PGS000607–PGS000616), the best discrimination evidence is for the UKB-evaluated GWAS-hits / P+T models with AUROC ~0.57 and strong per-SD effect sizes (OR ~1.28–1.30) plus strong top-1% vs rest enrichment. Between those, PGS000613 (P+T, 15 variants; DEV UKB n=12,992; EVAL n=13,530) slightly edges PGS000611 by AUROC (0.571 vs 0.567) and top-1% OR (2.91 vs 2.66), while keeping the same clean, basic covariate set (age, sex, batch, PCs). 

Alternatives with very high full-model AUROC (PGS000071/PGS000782 ~0.80) are heavily covariate-packaged (age/sex plus smoking variables and BMI), so that AUROC is likely dominated by non-genetic predictors and is not strong evidence of PRS quality; they also do not provide PRS-only AUC/R². Therefore, the best-supported PRS-centric choice in this fixed list is PGS000613, though confidence remains moderate because PRS-only discrimination metrics are not reported."}
